## Before you run it

Create a read-only key for the @@TITLE@@ API and put it in the environment as `@@ENV@@`. The key is read there and nowhere else: never from an argument or a file, and never written to a file, a log or a manifest.

## Commands

Each command takes `--data-dir`; `backfill` and `daily` read the key from `@@ENV@@` and take `--provider-account-id`, the identifier of the account the records are about. It is required and must not be empty (an empty one is exit 2). The run manifest keeps it, so `rebuild` needs no option.

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `backfill --since YYYY-MM-DD --provider-account-id ID` | Reads every closed UTC day from that day to yesterday and publishes the records. |
| `daily --provider-account-id ID [--since YYYY-MM-DD]` | Reads every closed day since the last day loaded, and at least the last 5 closed days (`REREAD_DAYS`), which may have been revised. With no `state.json` it stops (exit 2) and asks for `--since`; with a `state.json` it ignores `--since` and says so. |
| `rebuild` | Rebuilds `records/` from the stored raw responses and the run manifests. It makes no request and needs no key. |

Exit codes, which a run also reports as `failure_kind`:

| Code | `failure_kind` | Meaning | What to do |
|---|---|---|---|
| `0` | `none` | The run finished. | Nothing. |
| `1` | `transient` | A 5xx, a 429 after the retries, a timeout or a connection error. | Run the same command again later; progress is kept. |
| `2` | `input` | A failed check (including a repeated record key), bad arguments or a configuration error such as a missing key. | Fix the input; running the same command again fails the same way. |
| `3` | `permanent` | The provider refused the request: 400, 401, 403, 404 or 422. | Fix the key, its scope or the identifier the message names; do not retry unchanged. |

## How it works

The connector sends one query per UTC day and endpoint and follows the page cursor (`next_page`, sent back as `page`) until there is none. It keeps all the pages of a query in one file under `raw/`, each response as received, turns the rows into `UsageRecord` lines under `records/usage/`, and writes a run manifest under `runs/`. Before anything is written, `meterline.batch_failures` checks the records of each kind; a failed check stops the run (exit 2) and publishes nothing. Numbers are read as `Decimal` with `json.loads(..., parse_float=Decimal)`, in the one helper `load_json`, so a cost keeps all its digits. The state file `state.json` lists the days loaded and the run in progress. A record is published only when its content differs from the latest published record with the same key, so a re-read day adds nothing unless the provider revised it.

<!-- two-endpoints: begin -->
The `totals` report becomes `OrgTotal` lines under `records/org_totals/`, one per day and model.

<!-- two-endpoints: end -->
## Files under the data directory

The data directory holds the following, all readable by the owner only (files `0600`, folders `0700`):

- `raw/<endpoint>/<window>/<batch_id>.json.gz`: one file per query, for `usage` and any further endpoint, never overwritten. It is gzip JSON with the request URL, `pulled_at` and a list `pages` of `{status, body}`, where `body` is the response text as received. The window is the day, `YYYY-MM-DD`.
- `records/usage/<batch_id>.jsonl`: the records a run published.
<!-- two-endpoints: begin -->
- `records/org_totals/<batch_id>.jsonl`: the organisation totals a run published.
<!-- two-endpoints: end -->
- `runs/<batch_id>.json`: the run manifest, with the `provider_account_id` of the run and the `unknown_fields` the provider sent that the connector does not read.
- `state.json`: the days loaded and the run in progress.

## Record mapping

Each row of a day's report becomes one `usage` record. Replace this table with the mapping of the real API, one line per field.

| Provider field | Record field | Note |
|---|---|---|
| `user` | `actor_id`, `actor_email` | An email address. |
| `model` | `model` | As the provider names it. |
| `input_tokens` | `tokens_input_uncached` | |
| `output_tokens` | `tokens_output` | |
| `cost` | `cost` | A JSON number, read as a `Decimal` and kept as it is; it is never a float. The stub adds no rounding: if a provider sends more than 12 decimals (`meterline.MAX_DECIMALS`), round once, to that scale, in the normaliser. |
